  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Architectures of Resistance shows clear early reach through 1,623 recorded downloads and one scholarly citation. Its strongest signals point to policy- and practice-relevant work on sustainable cities and communities, with evidence of uptake in cultural studies and spatial scholarship.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2024, Architectures of Resistance: Negotiating Borders Through Spatial Practices enters a timely debate about how borders are made, maintained, and challenged through space. The book\u2019s topical focus aligns with sustainable cities and communities, and its concept profile connects architecture with politics, negotiation, and resistance, indicating a framing that is both spatial and socially engaged.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record shows active use soon after publication: 1,623 downloads were reported through OAPEN, suggesting substantial readership and interest beyond a purely symbolic presence. This kind of usage does not equate to citation-based scholarly uptake, but it is a meaningful signal that the book has found an audience engaging with its content.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still early but detectable. OpenAlex records one citation, from a 2025 article in the European Journal of Cultural Studies by Nirmal Puwar at Goldsmiths, University of London. That citing work sits in adjacent areas of urban planning, spatial studies, and cultural analysis, which is consistent with the book\u2019s interdisciplinary scope and its relevance to debates about spatial practice and border-making.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader support record also suggests institutional and scholarly investment in the book\u2019s themes: funding verification is associated with KU Leuven, University College London, Delft University of Technology, and Louisiana State University. Taken together, the evidence points to a book that is already being read and beginning to circulate in scholarly discourse, while the citation record remains too small for stronger claims about longer-term influence.</p>",
